.. highlight:: none

.. _syn-tools-storm-lint:

storm.lint
==========

The Synapse ``storm.lint`` tool checks Storm code for syntax errors, pipeline usage which may not do what was
intended, and Storm which could be written more simply. With ``--v3-ready`` it also reports Storm which must
change for Synapse 3.x. It parses the Storm locally and does not connect to a Cortex.

Syntax
------

``storm.lint`` is executed using ``python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint``. The command usage is as follows:

.. shell:: python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint -h

Each path may be:

- a Storm file, which is linted whatever its extension,
- a directory, which is searched recursively for ``*.storm`` files, or
- ``-``, which reads Storm code from stdin.

For example, to lint every Storm file in a Power-Up and a query read from stdin:

.. code-block:: bash

    python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint acme-hello/storm/
    echo '$x = $lib.list()' | python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint -

Output
------

Each finding is printed as ``<path>:<line>:<column>: <rule> <message>``, followed by a count of the findings:

.. code-block:: text

    example.storm:1:6: S005 Use a list literal such as ([]) instead of $lib.list().
    example.storm:3:13: P001 Avoid spin, isolate the query in a function.
    Found 2 errors.

When there are no findings, the tool prints ``All checks passed!``.

JSON Output
~~~~~~~~~~~

``--format json`` prints each finding as one JSON object per line, with ``path``, ``line``, ``column``,
``rule``, ``severity`` and ``mesg`` keys, and no summary line. A file which could not be read is printed as an
object with ``path`` and ``error`` keys. When there are no findings, nothing is printed:

.. code-block:: text

    python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint --v3-ready --format json example.storm
    {"path":"example.storm","line":1,"column":6,"rule":"R001","severity":"high","mesg":"$lib.true was removed, use (true)."}
    {"path":"example.storm","line":2,"column":1,"rule":"M001","severity":"high","mesg":"inet:ipv4 is renamed to inet:ip in 3.x."}

The exit codes are the same for both formats. A usage or configuration error is printed as text.
``--show-rules`` always prints text.

Exit Codes
----------

.. list-table::
    :header-rows: 1
    :widths: 10 90

    * - Code
      - Meaning
    * - ``0``
      - No findings.
    * - ``1``
      - One or more findings.
    * - ``2``
      - A usage error, an invalid configuration, an unknown rule ID, or a file which could not be read.

Selecting Rules
---------------

Three options choose which rules run:

- ``--select`` is exclusive: it replaces the default rule set, so only the selected rules run. Without it,
  every rule which is on by default is run.
- ``--enable`` is additive: it adds rules to the selected set, which is mostly used to turn on rules which are
  off by default.
- ``--ignore`` removes rules from the selected set.

Each option takes a comma-separated list of rule IDs or ID prefixes. A prefix matches every rule whose ID starts
with it, so ``S`` matches every style rule and ``S01`` matches ``S010`` through ``S016``. A rule named by
``--select`` or ``--enable`` runs whether or not it is on by default.

``--v3-ready`` adds every ``M`` rule and every ``R`` rule other than ``R004`` to the selected set, after
``--select`` and ``--enable`` and before ``--ignore``. ``--no-v3-ready`` turns it off when the configuration turns
it on. See
:ref:`syn-tools-storm-lint-v3-ready`.

.. code-block:: bash

    # only the 3.x syntax and runtime rules
    python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint --select R mypkg/storm/

    # the default rules, plus S010, S015 and S016 which are off by default
    python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint --enable S010,S015,S016 mypkg/storm/

    # the default rules, except the style rules
    python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint --ignore S mypkg/storm/

    # the default rules and the 3.x rules, except the data model rules
    python -m synapse.tools.storm.lint --v3-ready --ignore M mypkg/storm/

``--show-rules`` prints every rule with its severity, whether it is on by default, its name and the first line of
its description, and exits. The ``DEFAULT`` column is ``yes`` or ``no``, or ``v3`` for a rule which is added by
``--v3-ready``.

Suppressing Findings
--------------------

A ``// noqa`` comment suppresses findings reported on its line. A ``// noqa: <ID>`` comment suppresses only the
named rules, and takes a comma-separated list of rule IDs or ID prefixes:

.. code-block:: text

    $x = $lib.true            // noqa
    $y = $lib.dict()          // noqa: S005
    $z = $lib.list($lib.null) // noqa: R001, S005

A parse failure (``E001``) is always reported and cannot be suppressed.

Configuration
-------------

Default options may be set in a ``[tool.stormlint]`` table in a ``pyproject.toml`` file. Without ``--config``,
the tool uses the nearest ``pyproject.toml`` with a ``[tool.stormlint]`` table, searching upward from the current
directory. ``--config <path>`` reads the table from the given ``pyproject.toml`` instead.

.. code-block:: toml

    [tool.stormlint]
    select = ["E", "P", "S"]
    ignore = ["S002"]
    enable = ["S015"]
    v3_ready = true
    exclude = [
        "tests/files/*",
        "storm/vendored.storm",
    ]

.. list-table::
    :header-rows: 1
    :widths: 15 85

    * - Key
      - Description
    * - ``select``
      - Rule IDs or prefixes to run, in place of the default rules. Only the selected rules run.
    * - ``ignore``
      - Rule IDs or prefixes to skip.
    * - ``enable``
      - Rule IDs or prefixes to add to the selected rules, such as rules which are off by default.
    * - ``v3_ready``
      - A boolean. When ``true``, the rules which report Storm that must change for Synapse 3.x are added to the
        selected rules, as with ``--v3-ready``.
    * - ``exclude``
      - Glob patterns, relative to the directory of the ``pyproject.toml``, for files and directories which are
        not linted.

Each value other than ``v3_ready`` is a list of strings. A command line option replaces the configured value for
the same key rather than adding to it, and ``--v3-ready`` or ``--no-v3-ready`` replaces ``v3_ready``. An unknown
key or a value of the wrong type is a configuration error.

Rules
-----

Rule IDs are grouped by prefix: ``E`` rules report errors, ``P`` rules report pipeline usage which may not do
what was intended, ``S`` rules report Storm which could be written more simply, ``R`` rules report Storm which
does not run as intended on Synapse 3.x, and ``M`` rules report data model names which are renamed or removed in
Synapse 3.x. The ``M`` rules and every ``R`` rule other than ``R004`` are off by default, and are added by
``--v3-ready``. ``R004`` runs by default, since a bare ``true``, ``false`` or ``null`` is also a string on 2.x.

.. list-table::
    :header-rows: 1
    :widths: 8 18 9 9 56

    * - ID
      - Name
      - Severity
      - Default
      - Description
    * - ``E001``
      - ``parse-failure``
      - high
      - yes
      - The Storm code failed to parse. This finding is always reported and cannot be suppressed.
    * - ``E002``
      - ``invalid-escape``
      - high
      - yes
      - A double-quoted or backtick string contains a backslash escape which is not a valid Python string escape.
        Single-quoted strings do not process escapes and are not checked.
    * - ``E003``
      - ``duplicate-function``
      - high
      - yes
      - A function is defined more than once in the same scope.
    * - ``P001``
      - ``spin``
      - low
      - yes
      - The ``spin`` command discards the nodes in the pipeline. Isolate the query whose nodes are not needed in a
        function which uses ``return()`` or a ``fini`` block.
    * - ``P002``
      - ``spin-return``
      - medium
      - yes
      - ``spin | return(...)`` runs the return after consuming every node. Use ``return(...)`` when only one node
        can flow, or ``fini { return(...) }`` when more than one can.
    * - ``S001``
      - ``guid-seed``
      - medium
      - yes
      - A guid form node is added with a value from ``$lib.guid()``. Use a list seed such as
        ``[ form=(a, $b) ]``, or ``[ form=* ]`` for a random guid. The resulting guid may differ, so verify it
        before changing existing code.
    * - ``S002``
      - ``unnecessary-parens``
      - low
      - yes
      - Parens open an expression. Inside one which is already open, a literal, variable, call, or nested dict or
        list needs no parens of its own, and a lone variable assigned, passed as an argument or returned needs
        none either: ``($y)`` is just ``$y``. Parens are still required around a literal value such as
        ``(true)`` or ``(10)``, a dict or list literal used as a value, and grouping which changes precedence.
        Grouping a compound sub-expression is never reported.
    * - ``S003``
      - ``edge-yield``
      - low
      - yes
      - An edge edit uses a subquery which only yields a variable. Use the variable directly, such as
        ``+(refs)> $v`` instead of ``+(refs)> { yield $v }``.
    * - ``S004``
      - ``return-null``
      - low
      - yes
      - ``return((null))`` and ``return($lib.null)`` return the same value as ``return()``.
    * - ``S005``
      - ``lib-list-dict``
      - low
      - yes
      - ``$lib.list()`` and ``$lib.dict()`` should be list and dict literals such as ``([])`` and ``({})``.
    * - ``S006``
      - ``print-kwargs``
      - low
      - yes
      - ``$lib.print()`` and ``$lib.warn()`` format their message with kwargs. Use a backtick format string.
    * - ``S008``
      - ``guarded-for``
      - low
      - yes
      - An ``if`` statement which only guards a ``for`` loop over the variable it tests. An empty or null value
        iterates zero times, so the guard is not needed. The guard is only redundant when no node is in the
        pipeline, because a ``for`` loop over an empty or null value drops the inbound node. Verify it before
        changing existing code.
    * - ``S010``
      - ``guarded-prop-set``
      - low
      - no
      - An ``if`` statement which only sets a property to the variable it tests. Use ``?=`` which skips a null
        value. The ``if`` statement also skips a value which is not null but is false, such as ``0``, an empty
        string or an empty list, while ``?=`` sets it, and ``?=`` also skips a value which fails to norm. Verify
        the value can not be one of those before changing existing code.
    * - ``S011``
      - ``subquery-lift-value``
      - low
      - yes
      - A property is set from a subquery which only lifts a form by its primary value. Set the property to the
        value directly when the property is the form type.
    * - ``S012``
      - ``lift-filter``
      - low
      - yes
      - A query lifts a form and then filters on one of its properties by a value which does not read the node.
        Lift by the property instead.
    * - ``S014``
      - ``try-cast``
      - low
      - yes
      - A ``try`` block calls ``$lib.cast()``. Use ``$lib.trycast()`` and check its result instead.
    * - ``S015``
      - ``return-import``
      - medium
      - no
      - A function returns a module from ``$lib.import()``. In a privsep module the returned module inherits root
        privileges, and binding the import as ``$__name`` does not prevent that. Never return an imported module.
    * - ``S016``
      - ``print-http-resp``
      - medium
      - no
      - An HTTP response or ``$lib.axon.wget()`` result is printed or logged. It includes the request headers, which
        may carry credentials such as an API key. Print fields such as its code and reason instead. The rule follows
        variables assigned from ``$lib.inet.http`` calls and ``$lib.axon.wget()`` or ``$lib.axon.wput()`` in the same
        scope, until the variable is assigned something else. An assignment in an ``if``, ``try``, ``switch``, loop,
        subquery or empty block only ends it for a print in the same block. A parameter of a function defined in the
        same query is followed when any call to the function passes it a response. Suppress a finding for an object
        which is known to hold no credentials with a ``noqa`` comment.
    * - ``R001``
      - ``lib-constant``
      - high
      - v3
      - ``$lib.true``, ``$lib.false`` and ``$lib.null`` were removed in 3.x. Use ``(true)``, ``(false)`` and
        ``(null)``.
    * - ``R002``
      - ``gtor-call``
      - high
      - v3
      - ``$node.value``, ``$node.form``, ``$node.ndef``, ``$lib.version.synapse`` and ``$lib.version.commit`` are
        gtors in 3.x and can not be called. Drop the parentheses.
    * - ``R003``
      - ``node-iden``
      - high
      - v3
      - ``$node.iden`` was removed in 3.x.
    * - ``R004``
      - ``bare-constant``
      - medium
      - yes
      - A bare ``true``, ``false`` or ``null`` used as an assignment value, call argument, return value or
        function argument default is the string ``"true"``, ``"false"`` or ``"null"``. Use ``(true)``,
        ``(false)`` or ``(null)``, or quote the string.
    * - ``R005``
      - ``removed-api``
      - high
      - v3
      - A ``$lib`` API which was removed in 3.x is used, such as ``$lib.str``, ``$lib.text``, ``$lib.user``,
        ``$lib.ps``, ``$lib.bytes.put()`` or the ``$lib.gen`` helpers. The message names the replacement when
        there is one. ``$lib.vars``, ``$lib.globals``, ``$lib.env`` and user vars (``$user.vars``) are dict-like
        in 3.x, so calling ``get()``, ``set()``, ``pop()``, ``list()`` and similar methods on them is reported.
        Read and assign ``$lib.globals.<name>``, assign ``$lib.undef`` to delete, and iterate with a ``for``
        loop.
    * - ``R006``
      - ``deref-space``
      - high
      - v3
      - Whitespace before the ``.`` of a variable deref or a tag name segment, such as ``($foo .bar)`` or
        ``$(#foo .bar)``. 3.x does not allow whitespace before the dot, and reads a dot which follows whitespace
        as a virtual property. Whitespace after the dot is allowed.
    * - ``R007``
      - ``pivot-in-prop``
      - high
      - v3
      - An inbound pivot or join from a named form, such as ``<- edge:refs``. 3.x removed it, and the
        ``edge:*`` and ``graph:*`` forms it pivots from are light edges in 3.x, so use a walk such as
        ``<(refs)- *``.
    * - ``R008``
      - ``dotted-prop``
      - high
      - v3
      - A prop name with a dotted segment which is not a universal prop, such as ``:foo.bar`` or
        ``inet:dns:a.newp``. 3.x reads the dotted segment as a virtual property.
    * - ``M001``
      - ``renamed-form``
      - high
      - v3
      - A form which is renamed in 3.x is lifted, filtered, pivoted to or added, such as ``inet:ipv4``
        (``inet:ip``), ``hash:*`` (``crypto:hash:*``) or ``it:prod:soft`` (``it:software``). A full prop name of
        the form is reported too.
    * - ``M002``
      - ``removed-form``
      - high
      - v3
      - A form which is removed in 3.x is lifted, filtered, pivoted to or added, such as ``inet:ssl:cert``,
        ``inet:web:*`` or ``it:av:sig``. The message names the replacement when there is one.
    * - ``M003``
      - ``renamed-prop``
      - high
      - v3
      - A prop which is renamed or removed in 3.x is used, such as ``inet:dns:a:ipv4`` (``inet:dns:a:ip``),
        ``ou:org:orgtype`` (``ou:org:type``), ``risk:vuln:cve`` (``risk:vuln:id``) or the
        ``it:av:scan:result:target:*`` props (``it:av:scan:result:target``). The ``:ipv4``, ``:ipv6``,
        ``:ipv4s``, ``:ipv6s``, ``:net4`` and ``:net6`` props of forms which 3.x keeps are all reported, such as
        ``inet:server:ipv4`` (the ``.ip`` virtual prop of ``inet:server``). A relative prop is only checked when
        its form is known: in the edit block of a node add, or after a single-form lift which starts its query.
    * - ``M004``
      - ``univ-seen``
      - high
      - v3
      - The universal ``.seen`` prop is lifted, filtered, read, set or deleted. 3.x has no universal props, use
        ``:seen`` on forms which implement ``meta:observable``.

.. _syn-tools-storm-lint-v3-ready:

Checking Storm for Synapse 3.x
------------------------------

``--v3-ready``, or ``v3_ready = true`` in the configuration, adds the rules which report Storm that must change
before it runs on Synapse 3.x. The Storm is still parsed by the Synapse 2.x parser, so these rules report code
which runs on 2.x today:

- The ``R`` rules report Storm which fails, or which runs with a different meaning, on 3.x. These include the
  ``$lib`` constants and APIs which were removed, gtors which can no longer be called, bare ``true``, ``false``
  and ``null`` values, whitespace before a ``.`` deref, inbound pivots from a named form or prop, and dotted prop
  names which 3.x reads as virtual properties.
- The ``M`` rules report data model forms and props which are renamed or removed in 3.x, and the universal
  ``.seen`` prop. They check the form and prop names used in lifts, filters, pivots, node adds and property sets.
  A relative prop such as ``:ipv4`` is only checked when its form is known: inside the edit block of a node add,
  or after a single-form lift which starts its query. The message names the 3.x replacement when there is one.
  Storm which names a removed or renamed form or prop fails on 3.x, such as ``inet:ipv4=1.2.3.4`` or
  ``inet:fqdn +.seen``, so the data and the Storm must both be migrated.

The ``R`` and ``M`` findings have ``high`` or ``medium`` severity and fail the check like any other finding. Use
``--ignore M`` to skip the ``M`` rules, or ``--select M`` to list only them.

The ``R`` and ``M`` rules may also be run without ``--v3-ready``, by naming them with ``--select`` or
``--enable``.

.. note::

  The ``R`` and ``M`` rules cover the documented 3.x changes which can be found from Storm syntax alone. Review
  the Synapse 3.x change notes for changes which need more context, such as renamed Storm commands.

Linting from Python
-------------------

The ``synapse.lib.stormlint.StormLinter`` class lints Storm text directly. It takes the same ``select``,
``ignore`` and ``enable`` options as the tool, and a ``v3ready`` option which matches ``--v3-ready``. Its
``lint()`` method returns a list of ``Finding`` tuples, each with ``rule``, ``severity``, ``line``, ``column``
and ``mesg`` fields, sorted by line, column and rule:

.. code-block:: python

    import synapse.lib.stormlint as s_stormlint

    linter = s_stormlint.StormLinter(enable=('S015',), v3ready=True)
    for finding in linter.lint('$x = $lib.true'):
        print(finding.rule, finding.line, finding.column, finding.mesg)

By default the rules which check form and property names, such as ``S011`` and ``S012``, use the built-in data
model. Pass a data model dictionary, in the shape returned by the Cortex ``getModelDict()`` API, as ``model`` to
check names against that model instead, including its extended model forms and properties:

.. code-block:: python

    linter = s_stormlint.StormLinter(model=await core.getModelDict())

The Cortex MCP server's ``storm_lint`` tool lints a query this way against the Cortex's own data model. Its
``v3_ready`` option matches ``--v3-ready``.

``StormLinter`` raises ``BadArg`` for an unknown rule ID or prefix. ``lint()`` honors ``noqa`` comments; the
``[tool.stormlint]`` table is only read by the ``storm.lint`` tool.
